ng\",\"R4 late afternoon; personal order 3\",\"Close protagonist and Moss\",\"End the crossing cycle and force the pair to live in one reality.\",\"They emerge through an unstable mouth with depleted supplies.\",\"Secure the destination and relight a route before the field collapses.\",\"Without a working mouth they cannot return to R0 or choose another echo.\",\"The local field extinguishes and ordinary fire produces no reaction from the crying frame.\",\"Portal exhaustion, limited materials and approaching night.\",\"Attempt a relight, compare the Atlas recipe, inventory missing resources and move to the safest nearby shelf.\",\"R4 is habitable, but a full crying frame and anchor are the only recorded route away.\",\"Whether the Atlas method works and what R0 moment it would select remain unknown.\",\"Urgency to reluctant practical acceptance.\",\"Purple light drains from the frame while Moss waits at the cave mouth.\",\"The last ordinary relight attempt fails.\",\"The pair are stranded and must build a life near the seam.\",\"Can a place chosen by necessity become home?\",\"SP-06\",\"Structural transition from travel to settlement.\",\"Inert frame state, visible failed relight, depleted inventory, distinct R4 surface and no unsupported claim that crying obsidian accepts flint and steel.\"\n\"SC-08\",\"SQ-03\",\"R4 west-rim house, ravine and nearby resources\",\"R4 across several weeks; personal order 4\",\"Close protagonist and Moss\",\"Create a lived home, establish the rug fire route and show patient portal preparation.\",\"The pair have temporary shelter beside the dead mouth and no immediate route away.\",\"Build a safe shared life while gathering the return materials gradually.\",\"The later fire and activation need ordinary causes, while Moss's loss matters only if R4 has become home.\",\"The compact room places an open hearth near Moss's preferred warm floor, and crying obsidian is scarce.\",\"Survival resources, domestic layout and incomplete portal knowledge.\",\"Build stone around the flame, place a wool rug, feed Moss, use sit only for dangerous work, catalogue materials and sketch a blast baffle.\",\"The cold-open house, rug, final inventory plan and Moss routine all arise through normal play.\",\"The rug remains within a candidate fire-spread range and the final run will leave Moss there alone.\",\"Reluctance to humour, comfort and cautious future hope.\",\"Moss repeatedly settles on the wool as the crying-block stack grows beside the Atlas.\",\"The protagonist stops describing R4 shelter as temporary even while planning a route out.\",\"Home and return preparation now coexist in the same room.\",\"Which commitment will control him if the two become incompatible?\",\"SP-07;SP-08;SP-09\",\"Relationship, normality, fire setup and resource causality.\",\"Staged house progression, open-hearth geometry, real wool and wolf routine, portal-material continuity and version-specific fire testing before lock.\"\n\"SC-09\",\"SQ-03\",\"R4 house and conventional supply route\",\"R4 on the day of the fire\",\"Close protagonist and Moss\",\"Cause the protagonist's absence through the final portal requirement and preserve Moss's sit-state logic.\",\"Only the last crying obsidian and glowstone remain missing from the Atlas plan.\",\"Complete the inventory without taking Moss through the hazardous route.\",\"A complete supply allows eventual controlled activation, while the route risks ordinary mob and lava exposure.\",\"The familiar safe waiting place is the wool rug beside a fireplace assumed to be contained.\",\"The material objective, hazardous route and hidden flammability.\",\"Feed Moss, issue the sit command, check the stone surround, leave the permanent flame and make the final run.\",\"Moss is deliberately left somewhere the protagonist has repeatedly treated as safe.\",\"The exact moment the wool catches and Moss's eventual fate remain unseen.\",\"Domestic confidence to quiet foreboding.\",\"The door closes; the camera remains on Moss and the wool after footsteps fade.\",\"A small fire spread begins after the protagonist is gone.\",\"He returns carrying the materials seen in the cold open while the house has progressed into danger.\",\"Will he reach Moss before the shared room is consumed?\",\"PAY-08;PAY-09\",\"Absence cause, final inventory origin and fire ignition setup.\",\"Conventional route shorthand, continuous inventory, sitting wolf, candidate loaded-area design and exact fire-spread timing subject to testing.\"\n\"SC-10\",\"SQ-04\",\"R4 burning house, burned clearing and surrounding paths\",\"R4 night; personal order 5\",\"Close protagonist\",\"Complete the cold open, establish Moss's absence and turn grief into the final objective.\",\"The protagonist sees smoke while returning with the completed portal inventory.\",\"Reach Moss, then search every plausible nearby route.\",\"The film must exhaust immediate care before the protagonist chooses cross-reality reversal.\",\"The fire has consumed the wool position, the room is empty and no response comes from the clearing.\",\"Elapsed fire, Moss's sit state, collapsed sightlines and uncertainty.\",\"Drop supplies, repeat the water and wall break, inspect the room, call outside, search paths and trace the burn fan back to the hearth.\",\"The rug caught from the open flame, Moss is gone and the final portal materials survived outside after being dropped.\",\"Whether Moss died or otherwise ceased to be present in R4 remains unconfirmed.\",\"Panic to disbelief, exhaustive care, grief and dangerous resolve.\",\"The camera continues beyond SC-01's cut to an empty burned rug position, then follows the protagonist's search.\",\"The Atlas diagram becomes the only evidence of a reality where Moss is certainly alive.\",\"He carries the book and materials towards the ravine.\",\"Can he open the route and survive its direct activation?\",\"PAY-07;PAY-08;PAY-11;SP-13\",\"Cold-open resolution, fire causality, moral clarity and climax motivation.\",\"Matched fire blocking, empty wolf position, exterior search, burned-state reset, no corpse or invented token and no claim of tested fire progression.\"\n\"SC-11\",\"SQ-05\",\"R4 north-wall ravine cave\",\"R4 before dawn; personal order 6\",\"Close protagonist\",\"Pay off the builder mystery and assemble every earlier clue into the final mechanism.\",\"The protagonist has the Atlas, full material inventory and no response from Moss.\",\"Construct a keyed crying frame with enough protection to cross after discharge.\",\"The first threshold is the only documented reality in which Moss is unquestionably alive.\",\"The Atlas requires direct interaction and its final safe-distance measurement is missing.\",\"Grief-driven urgency opposed by incomplete blast knowledge.\",\"Place each crying block at the remembered coordinates, install and charge the anchor, then build the best stone baffle the cave permits.\",\"The protagonist is the material builder of the connected R0 portal and had understood the blast risk before acting.\",\"Whether the baffle is sufficient and whether activation precedes damage remain unknown.\",\"Purposeful construction to visible doubt, then commitment.\",\"Opening portal close-ups recur in reverse as the frame and surrounding geometry are created.\",\"He spends the final glowstone charge and takes the direct activation position.\",\"The frame is complete, inert and one interaction from the climax.\",\"Will the route form before the discharge kills him?\",\"PAY-02;PAY-05;PAY-06;PAY-09\",\"Title resolution, material continuity and earned climax setup.\",\"Full crying-frame build, exact coordinate match, charged-anchor prop, blast baffle and separation of candidate vanilla behaviour from fictional portal response.\"\n\"SC-12\",\"SQ-05\",\"R4 crying-frame chamber\",\"R4 before dawn; personal order 6\",\"Close protagonist, then fixed observer\",\"Resolve the final choice, death and portal activation through one causal event.\",\"The protagonist stands within direct interaction range of the charged anchor.\",\"Discharge the anchor, survive until the frame forms and cross.\",\"This is his last available action towards a reality where Moss is alive.\",\"The direct-hand rule exposes him to an unverified explosion radius.\",\"The protagonist's own urgent objective and the activation mechanism.\",\"Use the stone baffle, face the portal plane and trigger the anchor despite incomplete safety evidence.\",\"The explosion kills him, but its discharge successfully wakes the crying frame afterwards.\",\"No survival, consciousness transfer or Moss outcome is established.\",\"Hope to shock, silence and tragic mechanical success.\",\"The blast whites out the frame; purple light gathers only after the protagonist is gone.\",\"Activation succeeds in the wrong order for its builder.\",\"An active portal remains in R4 with no living traveller to enter it.\",\"Where did the keyed connection open?\",\"PAY-10\",\"Earned climax, protagonist agency and activation answer.\",\"Target-version anchor test, controlled death framing, frame-survival plan, fictional light effect and an observer camera that does not imply protagonist survival.\"\n\"SC-13\",\"SQ-05\",\"Portal passage, R0 underground layers and R0 clearing\",\"R0 morning at the moment of SC-02; presented after R4 death\",\"Omniscient audience camera, then earlier close protagonist and Moss\",\"Reveal the final destination, reinterpret the beginning and close the loop.\",\"The R4 frame is active after the protagonist's death.\",\"Follow the connection and identify the reality it has selected.\",\"The title, Atlas rule and requested loop remain incomplete until the destination is visible.\",\"The route passes through matching underground coordinates before any human figure appears.\",\"Cross-reality geography and audience uncertainty.\",\"Move through the portal, rise through stone and soil, match the SC-02 surface composition and continue the earlier moment.\",\"The frame opens into R0 at the first threshold; the earlier protagonist has the clean journal and will investigate the hum below.\",\"The exact counterpart history that produced the Atlas and Moss's R4 fate remain open.\",\"Mystery to recognition, tenderness and tragic inevitability.\",\"The camera breaks through grass behind Moss as the exact morning arrival resumes.\",\"The repeated line and blank journal identify this as Return to the beginning from the far side of the final portal.\",\"The earlier pair choose to stay above the mouth that will begin their journey.\",\"Can any later echo of the blank journal discover a safer answer, or is next the only direction?\",\"PAY-01;PAY-03;PAY-12;OPEN-01\",\"Destination reveal, closing image, journal pay-off and emotional completion.\",\"Stylised through-ground transition, precise surface match, repeated wolf blocking, clean journal and reused audio; no claim that this camera represents a surviving character.\"", ".studio/internal/story/information-map.csv": "info_id,question_or_fact,audience_knows_before,audience_expects_before,character_knowledge_before,delivery_beat_or_scene,audience_knows_after,audience_expects_after,emotional_orientation_after,withheld_or_revealed,fairness_setup_ids,payoff_or_resolution_id,notes\n\"INF-01\",\"What happened to Moss in the cold-open fire?\",\"The protagonist returns to a burning shared home and directs every action towards Moss before the scene cuts.\",\"Moss may still be in the room and reachable.\",\"The protagonist knows Moss was left sitting on the rug but has not yet seen the room.\",\"SC-10\",\"The sitting place is empty, the surrounding search fails and Moss is gone from observable R4.\",\"The protagonist will seek a reality in which Moss is certainly alive, but the exact R4 fate may remain unseen.\",\"Loss, uncertainty and sympathy without a false property dilemma.\",\"withheld\",\"SP-11|SP-13\",\"OPEN-01\",\"The film supplies absence rather than a corpse, collar object or unshown claim of survival.\"\n\"INF-02\",\"Where does the first portal crossing lead?\",\"The active frame sits beneath a recognisable R0 ravine.\",\"It may lead to the Nether, another location or another state of the same world.\",\"The protagonist has no tested model and expects ordinary portal logic to be relevant.\",\"SC-04\",\"Matching granite and coordinates exist beneath incompatible natural history in R1.\",\"Re-entry appears likely to return to R0 until the next transition disproves it.\",\"Disorientation mixed with spatial recognition.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-01|SP-02\",\"PAY-01\",\"Geography establishes place before the story names realities.\"\n\"INF-03\",\"Are the destinations different realities rather than changing times or resets?\",\"R1 and R2 share geography but have incompatible water and surface states.\",\"The portal may be cycling one mutable world.\",\"The protagonist suspects an ordered chain but lacks a persistent test.\",\"SC-05\",\"A cobblestone mark placed beside one mouth is absent beside the next while coordinates and frame geometry persist.\",\"The audience expects stable echoes with local block histories.\",\"Methodical recognition replaces arbitrary uncertainty.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-01|SP-04\",\"PAY-04\",\"The Atlas later labels a model already supported by action.\"\n\"INF-04\",\"Why can the protagonist not simply go back or keep crossing?\",\"The first return attempt advances and successive mouths follow the same pattern.\",\"A later mouth might eventually cycle back to R0.\",\"The protagonist knows only that each observed crossing has moved forwards.\",\"SC-06;SC-07\",\"The Atlas records a forward-only unanchored chain, and the R4 mouth closes before another crossing is possible.\",\"A deliberately keyed full frame becomes the only proposed route to the first threshold.\",\"Knowledge provides hope while stranding becomes real.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-04|SP-05|SP-06\",\"PAY-06\",\"The rule is fictional canon and remains subject to human approval.\"\n\"INF-05\",\"Who created the Weeping Atlas?\",\"The protagonist carries a clean journal with a distinctive ravine symbol and repaired corner.\",\"Any portal explanation may come from an unknown prior traveller.\",\"He knows his own notation and has not seen another person.\",\"SC-06;SC-13\",\"The Archive book is an older counterpart of his journal, and the final R0 image repeats its clean beginning.\",\"Another echo of the protagonist is the strongest inference, but its exact experimental history remains open.\",\"Personal uncanniness rather than external lore exposition.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-03\",\"PAY-03\",\"No counterpart appears or speaks; only the document and final prop support the inference.\"\n\"INF-06\",\"What starts the house fire?\",\"The house has a stone-surrounded open hearth and Moss routinely sits on a wool rug beside it.\",\"The stone may appear to make the fireplace safe.\",\"The protagonist believes the permanent flame is contained.\",\"SC-09;SC-10\",\"Fire spreads from the open hearth to wool within range, then from the rug into the timber room while he is away.\",\"The audience can trace the cold-open burn fan without an echo force or random explosion.\",\"Tragic recognition of an affectionate practical mistake.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-07|SP-08\",\"PAY-07\",\"Exact spread distance and simulation behaviour require target-version testing.\"\n\"INF-07\",\"How can a complete crying-obsidian frame behave differently?\",\"The observed frame already connects realities and ignores ordinary reverse travel.\",\"Its behaviour may be uncontrollable.\",\"The protagonist has verified only the unanchored next-echo rule.\",\"SC-06;SC-11\",\"The Atlas proposes that a full frame keyed by direct respawn-anchor discharge reaches the traveller's first threshold.\",\"The final build will test the method under lethal uncertainty.\",\"Qualified hope and concern.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-05|SP-10\",\"PAY-05\",\"The story labels this rule as invented rather than vanilla Minecraft.\"\n\"INF-08\",\"Why is the protagonist away when the rug catches fire?\",\"He has been collecting portal materials gradually while living with Moss.\",\"A final journey may complete the Atlas method.\",\"He believes Moss is safer sitting at home than travelling through the hazardous supply route.\",\"SC-09\",\"The final run supplies the crying obsidian and glowstone dropped in the cold open and leaves Moss beside the vulnerable rug.\",\"Those same materials can now drive an immediate grief response.\",\"Foreboding grounded in a responsible-looking choice.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-08|SP-09\",\"PAY-09\",\"The route and loaded-area behaviour remain production questions.\"\n\"INF-09\",\"What causes the final explosion and death?\",\"The Atlas shows a charged anchor, direct-hand keying and a torn blast-distance page.\",\"The protagonist may construct enough protection to survive and cross.\",\"He knows the activation is dangerous but lacks the final safety measurement.\",\"SC-12\",\"His direct discharge produces the explosion, kills him and wakes the fictional portal field after his death.\",\"The audience expects the active frame to reveal a destination without carrying him through.\",\"Shock followed by tragic mechanical satisfaction.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-09|SP-10\",\"PAY-10\",\"Candidate anchor behaviour requires verification; crying-frame activation remains explicit invention.\"\n\"INF-10\",\"Who made the initial portal, and where does the final frame lead?\",\"The R0 mouth has no local builder and the opening surface scene appears to precede all portal events.\",\"The protagonist may build a similar gate without proving it is the same connection.\",\"He believes the full frame will seek his first threshold but cannot observe its result before death.\",\"SC-11;SC-13\",\"The protagonist materially builds the R4 frame, and the camera emerges into the exact R0 arrival above its projected receiving mouth.\",\"The earlier pair will descend, enter and begin the same one-way journey.\",\"Mystery resolution, tenderness and loop recognition.\",\"revealed\",\"SP-02|SP-05|SP-12\",\"PAY-12\",\"Nobody built the frame locally in R0; its constructed source is the connected R4 frame.\"", ".studio/internal/story/setup-payoff-ledger.csv": "setup_id,setup_beat_or_scene,setup,initial_audience_interpretation,payoff_id,payoff_beat_or_scene,payoff,planned_payoff_type,resolution_status,intentionally_open_reason,latest_acceptable_point,notes\n\"SP-01\",\"SC-02;SC-04\",\"The R0 and R1 ravines share a lightning-shaped granite seam, stone rib and coordinate pattern despite incompatible surface states.\",\"The portal may have moved the pair through time or altered one world.\",\"PAY-01\",\"SC-05;SC-13\",\"Missing local marks establish separate echoes, and the final ascent uses the same coordinates to reveal R0 as the destination.\",\"reinterpretation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-13\",\"Use exact terrain and lenses before relying on narration.\"\n\"SP-02\",\"SC-03\",\"A complete active crying-obsidian frame stands in an unvisited R0 cave without a local approach route or builder evidence.\",\"An unknown traveller or anomaly may have constructed it.\",\"PAY-02\",\"SC-11;SC-13\",\"The protagonist builds the connected full frame in R4, whose receiving mouth appears in R0.\",\"confirmation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-13\",\"The R0 frame is projected from a material source rather than locally placed by another character.\"\n\"SP-03\",\"SC-02\",\"The protagonist records the clearing in a clean survey journal using a distinctive ravine symbol and repaired corner.\",\"It is ordinary expedition bookkeeping.\",\"PAY-03\",\"SC-06;SC-13\",\"The Archive Echo contains an older counterpart with the same notation, and the final image restores the clean journal at its beginning.\",\"reinterpretation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-13\",\"The exact counterpart history may remain open, but the prop identity must be unmistakable.\"\n\"SP-04\",\"SC-04;SC-05\",\"Re-entering advances to R2, and a cobblestone mark placed beside one mouth is absent at the next.\",\"The portal may be malfunctioning or randomly selecting worlds.\",\"PAY-04\",\"SC-05;SC-06\",\"Observed marks and Atlas arrows establish a stable forward-only echo chain.\",\"confirmation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-06\",\"Keep the mark outside the frame so its absence tests local reality rather than portal movement.\"\n\"SP-05\",\"SC-03;SC-06\",\"Crying particles flow directionally, and the Atlas labels the system one frame, many mouths with a full-frame route to first threshold.\",\"The labels may be an unverified theory from an unseen traveller.\",\"PAY-05\",\"SC-11;SC-12;SC-13\",\"The completed frame and anchor discharge activate a connection whose far mouth is the protagonist's first R0 threshold.\",\"confirmation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-13\",\"Fictional rule requires human canon approval and consistent visual language.\"\n\"SP-06\",\"SC-07\",\"The R4 mouth collapses and cannot be relit with ordinary fire, forcing the pair to remain near its coordinates.\",\"The portal chain may have exhausted or the frame may be damaged.\",\"PAY-06\",\"SC-11\",\"The protagonist returns to the same seam and builds the full keyed frame instead of attempting another ordinary relight.\",\"escalation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-11\",\"Make the missing materials and failed relight visible.\"\n\"SP-07\",\"SC-08\",\"A wool rug is placed beside a stone-surrounded open fireplace where Moss prefers to sit.\",\"The stone surround makes the warm domestic arrangement appear safe.\",\"PAY-07\",\"SC-09;SC-10\",\"The open flame reaches wool within its spread range and the rug carries fire into the timber room while the protagonist is away.\",\"tragic_fulfilment\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-10\",\"Exact geometry and fire-tick behaviour require target-version testing.\"\n\"SP-08\",\"SC-08;SC-09\",\"Moss routinely waits on the rug only when the protagonist undertakes dangerous work and follows again on return.\",\"The sit command is responsible care and ordinary household routine.\",\"PAY-08\",\"SC-10\",\"The same command state prevents following or teleporting during the fire, and the sitting place is empty when the protagonist reaches it.\",\"tragic_fulfilment\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-10\",\"Moss is never penned or placed in a separate pet room.\"\n\"SP-09\",\"SC-01;SC-08;SC-09\",\"Crying obsidian and glowstone are accumulated slowly, and the cold open shows the completed supply dropping outside the fire.\",\"The materials are connected to the unexplained portal but not yet to the protagonist's absence.\",\"PAY-09\",\"SC-11;SC-12\",\"The same inventory completes the frame and charged anchor immediately after the failed search.\",\"confirmation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-12\",\"Show ordinary acquisition or concise survival shorthand and preserve quantities.\"\n\"SP-10\",\"SC-06\",\"The Atlas requires the activating hand to discharge the anchor, but its final blast-distance measurement is torn away.\",\"The danger may be manageable with improvised cover.\",\"PAY-10\",\"SC-11;SC-12\",\"The direct-hand requirement keeps the protagonist inside the lethal blast problem and the incomplete baffle fails.\",\"tragic_fulfilment\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-12\",\"Do not imply that the book guaranteed survival.\"\n\"SP-11\",\"SC-01\",\"The cold open cuts as the protagonist breaks towards the unseen shared room.\",\"A direct rescue may still reach Moss.\",\"PAY-11\",\"SC-10\",\"The repeated action continues to the consumed rug position, where Moss is gone, followed by an unsuccessful external search.\",\"tragic_fulfilment\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-10\",\"Withhold through editing rather than false narration.\"\n\"SP-12\",\"SC-02\",\"Moss sits at the overlook while the protagonist opens his blank journal and decides that they can stay.\",\"This is a conventional chronological restart after the cold open.\",\"PAY-12\",\"SC-13\",\"The camera rises from the final portal into the exact scene, revealing it as the first-threshold destination and the next beginning of the loop.\",\"reinterpretation\",\"planned\",\"\",\"SC-13\",\"Match blocking, light, audio and journal position precisely.\"\n\"SP-13\",\"SC-10\",\"No body, invented collar object or on-screen death message establishes what happened to Moss after the fire began.\",\"Moss most likely died in the shared room, but absence leaves a narrow uncertainty.\",\"OPEN-01\",\"SC-13\",\"The ending shows Moss alive only in the earlier R0 reality and does not resolve the R4 wolf's exact fate.\",\"reinterpretation\",\"intentionally_open\",\"The requested event is that Moss is gone; keeping the R4 fate unseen avoids invented remains and preserves the protagonist's uncertainty without affecting the fire, portal, death or loop.\",\"SC-13\",\"Human approval should decide whether Story lock retains absence or requires explicit death evidence.\"", ".studio/internal/story/relationships.csv": "relationship_id,character_a,character_b,public_relationship,private_tension,shared_history,what_a_wants_from_b,what_b_wants_from_a,power_balance,secret,predicted_change\n\"REL-01\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"WOLF\",\"An established solo player and tamed wolf travel through successive echo-realities and turn forced settlement beside a ravine into a shared home.\",\"The protagonist's habit of seating Moss somewhere safe protects the wolf during ordinary danger, but the chosen wool-rug position becomes vulnerable to the fireplace while he is away.\",\"They arrive at R0 together, survey the clearing, cross every echo as a pair, share limited shelter, build R4 routines, gather resources and repeatedly return to the same warm rug beside the hearth.\",\"He wants Moss beside him through ordinary life, safely absent from hazardous supply travel and ultimately present in whichever reality becomes home.\",\"Expressed behaviourally, Moss wants proximity, food after damage, a clear follow state and the familiar warm resting place.\",\"The protagonist controls portal entry, route choice and sit-follow commands; after the fire, Moss's absence controls the protagonist's search and final willingness to risk death.\",\"The R0 portal they discover is the receiving mouth of the frame the protagonist builds only after Moss disappears in R4.\",\"Comfortable partnership becomes chosen home, then ambiguous loss and a fatal attempt to reach the earlier reality where the relationship is still intact.\"", ".studio/internal/story/continuity-bible.md": "# Continuity bible — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n## Canon authority\n\n- Canon owner: Luan\n- Current canon version: Story proposal 0.3 — unlocked, 16 July 2026\n- Locked upstream artefacts: None stated. The supplied Direction and prior Story plan are explicitly unlocked.\n- Status of this bible: proposed continuity for human review; it records no Story lock, audience result or production validation.\n- Major proposed changes: parallel echo-realities replace the fixed timeline; Moss accompanies the protagonist from the beginning; the initial and final portal uses crying obsidian; the Weeping Atlas establishes the invented rule; a wool rug beside an open hearth causes the fire; Moss becomes missing rather than visibly confirmed dead; and a respawn-anchor discharge causes the protagonist’s terminal explosion.\n- Rule for later change: portal canon, Moss’s fate, theme, motivation and structural changes require human approval before a creative lock.\n\n## Reality register\n\n| Reality | Stable geography | Divergent state | Required story event |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R0 — Root Echo | Clearing above the ravine, lightning-shaped granite seam, north-wall cave and west-rim shelf | Green surface, dry main wall and no local player construction above ground | The protagonist and Moss arrive, choose to stay, discover the projected crying portal and enter it. The final R4 frame later opens its receiving mouth into this moment. |\n| R1 — Rain Echo | Same seam, cave position and ravine profile | Persistent rain, added waterfall and dense vegetation | The pair explore; the protagonist re-enters expecting to return to R0. |\n| R2 — Ash Echo | Same coordinates and portal position | Naturally scorched tree line and exposed shelf | The attempted return arrives here. The protagonist begins controlled marking tests. |\n| R3 — Archive Echo | Same ravine anchors | Small abandoned survey recess beside the portal cave | The protagonist finds the Weeping Atlas, verifies its notation against his observations and takes it before the mouth destabilises. |\n| R4 — Home Echo | Same ravine anchors and west-rim build shelf | Habitable surface and an inert local mouth | The pair settle, build the house, experience the rug fire and loss, and construct the final keyed crying frame. |\n\nThe echoes are stable realities rather than resets, dreams or replacement timelines. They need not share a synchronised clock.\n\n## Personal order and audience order\n\n### Protagonist’s personal order\n\nP1 R0 arrival with Moss → P2 R0 portal discovery and entry → P3 R1 exploration → P4 attempted return into R2 → P5 marking tests → P6 R3 Atlas discovery → P7 final uncontrolled crossing into R4 → P8 R4 settlement and house → P9 final material run → P10 fire and Moss’s disappearance → P11 search and decision → P12 crying-frame construction → P13 respawn-anchor discharge and death.\n\n### Audience presentation\n\nA short fragment from P10 opens the film. The presentation then follows P1 through P13. After the protagonist dies, an omniscient camera crosses the active portal and rises into P1 in R0. The repeated scene continues far enough to confirm that the earlier pair will investigate the portal beneath them.\n\nThe final camera is audience access. It does not represent the protagonist’s soul, respawn or survival.\n\n## Crying-portal rules\n\nThese are proposed fictional rules and must not be described as vanilla Minecraft facts.\n\n1. A complete crying-obsidian frame can hold a portal plane.\n2. The frame binds matching horizontal coordinates across echo-realities. It selects the nearest safe vertical exit around the repeated ravine seam.\n3. Unanchored entry advances through the echo chain. Immediate re-entry does not reverse the last crossing.\n4. Player blocks beside a mouth remain local to their reality. The frame and natural coordinate anchors recur; an adjacent cobblestone mark does not.\n5. A projected receiving mouth may appear in a reality where nobody locally placed blocks. Its material source is the constructed keyed frame in another echo.\n6. The R0 mouth is the receiving mouth projected by the final R4 frame. Nobody builds it locally in R0.\n7. Entering the R0 receiving face without being the keyed builder starts the forward chain R0 → R1 → R2 → R3 → R4.\n8. The R4 arrival mouth collapses after the uncontrolled chain completes and cannot be relit with ordinary fire.\n9. A new complete frame can be keyed to its builder’s first threshold by directly discharging a charged respawn anchor within the frame geometry.\n10. Direct interaction is required because the activating hand supplies the traveller key. Remote activation would not select the first threshold in this fictional rule set.\n11. The proposed Overworld anchor discharge is explosive. The explosion candidate requires target-version verification; the frame’s activation response is invented.\n12. The discharge keys the R4 frame to R0 at the moment the protagonist and Moss first established the clearing as their threshold.\n13. Activation does not rewrite R0 or resurrect R4 Moss. It opens a connection to an already existing echo-state.\n14. After the protagonist’s death, the active R4 portal remains long enough for the camera to reveal its destination.\n\nNo invisible force corrects choices. Each reversal follows a portal rule, material adjacency, command state or decision already established.\n\n## The Weeping Atlas\n\n### Prop continuity\n\n- The protagonist’s clean survey journal has a green cloth repair at the lower-right corner.\n- His ravine notation uses three descending lines crossed by a short horizontal shelf mark.\n- The R0 opening insert shows the first clean version of that symbol.\n- The Archive Echo book uses the same cover repair, symbol, coordinate shorthand and hand but is older, water-damaged and fuller.\n- The protagonist carries both books out of R3; the clean journal may later be stored, but the Atlas remains available through the climax.\n- The final R0 surface reveal must show the clean journal, not the weathered Atlas.\n\n### Legible Atlas information\n\n- Repeated plans of the same ravine.\n- Forward arrows between mouths.\n- BACK crossed out and NEXT repeated.\n- ONE FRAME, MANY MOUTHS.\n- A complete crying frame connected to a charged respawn anchor.\n- FIRST THRESHOLD as the selected destination.\n- A hand symbol beside the activation point.\n- A blast warning whose final safe-distance measurement is torn away.\n\nThe film does not confirm which counterpart completed the Atlas or how that counterpart survived long enough to record the rule. The matching journal makes another echo of the protagonist the intended inference. This open history cannot be expanded into a speaking traveller, portal guardian or lore institution without approval.\n\n## Character states and knowledge\n\n| Scene | Protagonist state, knowledge and objective | Key possessions | Moss state |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| SC-01 | Presented late in personal order; knows Moss was left in the house and spends no action on property. | Water bucket, ordinary tools, final crying obsidian and glowstone supply. | Not visible from the doorway; fate withheld by the cut. |\n| SC-02 | Has no portal model; chooses the clearing as a possible home. | Clean survey journal, expedition tools, food and ordinary survival gear. | Tamed before the film, following normally and sitting briefly at the overlook. |\n| SC-03 | Knows the frame is active and locally impossible; chooses entry. | Same kit and journal; no Atlas. | Standing and following through the controlled threshold. |\n| SC-04 | Knows R1 shares coordinates but differs visibly; incorrectly expects re-entry to return to R0. | Carried kit, map and journal. | Following, unsettled only through ordinary movement and command response. |\n| SC-05 | Knows immediate return failed and begins systematic tests. | Cobblestone marker blocks and journal notes. | Kept near the protagonist during each crossing. |\n| SC-06 | Recognises the Atlas as a counterpart of his journal and learns the proposed keyed-frame method. | Clean journal plus weathered Atlas. | Waiting nearby; not used to interpret the book. |\n| SC-07 | Knows R4 is habitable and the local mouth is dead; lacks materials for the full method. | Depleted travel kit, both books and no complete crying inventory. | Alive, following and stranded with him. |\n| SC-08 | Builds a shared home and prepares slowly rather than risking an incomplete activation. | Growing crying-obsidian supply, glowstone, tools and baffle sketches. | Following during safe work, fed after damage and routinely sitting on the rug during danger. |\n| SC-09 | Believes the stone-surrounded hearth and familiar sit position are safe during the final run. | Supply kit taken out; Atlas remains secured or carried according to capture continuity. | Healthy enough to remain home, seated on the wool rug and not enclosed. |\n| SC-10 | Returns with the final materials, finds the house burning and searches for Moss. | Materials dropped outside, water and breaking tools; Atlas recoverable after the search. | Gone from the burned sitting position and surrounding searched area; exact fate unconfirmed. |\n| SC-11 | Believes R0 is the only documented reality where Moss is certainly alive and accepts incomplete blast evidence. | Full crying-frame inventory, charged anchor materials, Atlas and damaged post-fire equipment. | Absent from R4 viewpoint. |\n| SC-12 | Directly triggers the anchor and dies before the field forms. | Ordinary drops may remain, burn or be obscured; their final disposition is not a clue requirement. | Absent. |\n| SC-13 | Earlier R0 self knows only that the clearing is suitable and a hum comes from below. | Clean, mostly blank survey journal and original expedition kit. | Alive, following and sitting at the overlook before the first discovery. |\n\nNarration may state belief but not unsupported certainty. Before SC-10 the narrator cannot say Moss died. After SC-10 he may say Moss was gone or that he could not find Moss, but not that he witnessed death. Before SC-06 he cannot name echoes as established fact.\n\n## Relationship continuity\n\nThe protagonist and Moss begin as an established pair. No taming scene is required in this version.\n\nRequired ordinary interactions include:\n\n- Moss following into the R0 clearing;\n- waiting during map inspection;\n- crossing each portal under controlled blocking;\n- occupying an intended build position in R4;\n- receiving food after ordinary damage;\n- choosing the wool rug as a repeated warm resting place;\n- being seated only when the protagonist undertakes hazardous work;\n- being released promptly on ordinary returns before the final run.\n\nThe final sit command must read as a familiar safety routine, not confinement. Moss has no pen, kennel, doghouse or separate room.\n\nOnce the protagonist sees the fire, he does not recover portal materials, chests or decorative blocks before reaching the shared room. The materials survive only because he drops them immediately outside.\n\n## House and fire continuity\n\n### R4 house layout\n\n- The compact house occupies the west-rim shelf near the ravine seam.\n- The main room contains the bed, work area, stone-surrounded open hearth and wool rug.\n- Moss’s familiar sitting point is on the rug, not behind a door or fence.\n- Timber capable of carrying fire extends from the main-room floor or wall beyond the rug.\n- The shortest rescue route can be opened through one exterior wall.\n- Recoverable storage is visible but physically off the rescue line so ignoring it is legible.\n\n### Fire chain\n\n1. The protagonist constructs a stone surround but leaves an open permanent flame.\n2. He later places wool within the target version’s candidate fire-spread range.\n3. Moss repeatedly rests there, making the placement emotionally and visually memorable.\n4. Before the final supply run, the protagonist seats Moss on the rug and leaves the flame active.\n5. During his absence, fire reaches the wool and spreads into adjacent timber.\n6. He returns after multiple room surfaces are involved.\n7. He drops the portal materials, places water towards Moss and breaks through the shortest wall.\n8. The rug position is empty when reached.\n9. A search of the shell and nearby routes produces no response.\n10. The remaining burn fan points back to the hearth and missing wool.\n\nThe exact ignition distance, spread timing, chunk simulation and wolf state require target-version testing. If the selected setup is unreliable, geometry may change while retaining the user-approved cause: wool rug beside fireplace catches while the protagonist is away.\n\nMoss’s R4 fate is intentionally open at proposal stage. Do not add a corpse, collar drop, name-tag item or death message without approval.\n\n## Material and inventory continuity\n\n- The protagonist carries a clean survey journal from R0 through all echoes.\n- He takes the Weeping Atlas from R3 into R4.\n- Initial R4 supplies are insufficient for a full crying frame and charged anchor.\n- Crying obsidian and glowstone accumulate visibly during the R4 home period.\n- A final conventional supply run provides the remaining quantity.\n- The cold open shows those final materials being dropped outside the burning house.\n- They remain available after the failed search and are carried into the ravine.\n- The final frame and anchor must use no unexplained duplicate materials.\n- Exact quantities depend on the approved frame dimensions, anchor recipe and target edition; continuity counts must be fixed after research.\n\n## Ravine geography and repeated compositions\n\n- Every echo retains the lightning-shaped granite seam, north-wall cave, narrow stone rib and west-rim shelf.\n- R1 adds rain and a waterfall without changing the core rock silhouette.\n- R2 changes the surface history but preserves the coordinate anchors.\n- R3 adds the survey recess without introducing a visible resident.\n- R4 supports the house and final construction.\n- Portal-adjacent cobblestone marks must remain outside the crying frame so their disappearance tests local matter.\n- SC-02 and SC-13 use the same surface camera, light, Moss position, journal angle and spoken line.\n- SC-03 and SC-11 repeat crying-frame coordinates from opposite causal sides.\n- SC-01 and SC-10 repeat the dropped materials, water placement, ignored storage and wall break; SC-10 continues beyond the earlier cut.\n\n## Explosion and ending continuity\n\n- The protagonist knows the activation is explosive before building the final frame.\n- The Atlas requires direct interaction and lacks the final safe-distance measurement.\n- He constructs an incomplete stone baffle rather than ignoring protection altogether.\n- The anchor discharge occurs before the fictional purple field forms.\n- The explosion is terminal. No respawn, totem, escape frame or ambiguous survival shot is permitted in this proposal.\n- The portal activates after his death and remains stable for the audience camera.\n- The camera passes through the active plane, then rises vertically through corresponding underground layers.\n- The transition reaches R0 morning and the exact SC-02 composition.\n- The repeated narration belongs to the earlier moment; it is not spoken by a surviving R4 consciousness.\n- A faint portal hum beneath the final surface scene confirms that the earlier pair are about to discover the receiving mouth.\n\n## Voice and cast\n\n- Human voices: one narrator, voiced by the creator.\n- On-screen human avatars: one protagonist only.\n- Additional presence: Moss.\n- The Atlas counterpart never appears, speaks or supplies a second performance.\n- The narrator may be retrospective but cannot conceal established scene knowledge or state invented rules as vanilla facts.\n\n## Production questions and required evidence\n\nBefore Story lock, verify or decide:\n\n- target Minecraft edition, version, difficulty and fire-spread settings;\n- wolf transfer through the planned portal capture;\n- sit, follow and teleport continuity across the final supply absence;\n- fireplace-to-wool ignition distance;\n- rug-to-timber spread and required elapsed time;\n- simulation-distance or chunk-loading design during the material run;\n- ordinary acquisition route and exact quantities for crying obsidian and glowstone;\n- candidate respawn-anchor charging, direct interaction, explosion radius and player lethality;\n- whether the desired frame survives the practical blast staging;\n- visual separation between ordinary explosion behaviour and fictional portal activation;\n- legibility of the Atlas pages at final output resolution;\n- feasibility of five ravine states and exact matched compositions;\n- whether Moss’s absence is sufficient or explicit death evidence is desired at human lock;\n- whether the counterpart-journal origin remains acceptably open.\n\nNo fire test, wolf test, anchor test, animatic result, audience response, capture validation or production approval is claimed."}, "questions": [], "warnings": ["The crying-obsidian echo portal, projected receiving mouth and respawn-anchor keying are proposed fictional canon and require human approval before Story lock.", "The fireplace-to-wool spread, loaded-area timing, wolf command continuity and respawn-anchor blast geometry require edition- and version-specific evidence or reproduction testing.", "Moss is confirmed absent but not explicitly dead in R4. This is intentional in the proposal and should be decided at human lock.", "The Weeping Atlas is strongly implied to come from another echo of the protagonist, but that counterpart’s exact history remains intentionally open."]}
