{"summary":"Added the missing ending final-image decision and explicit production requirements for SC-01 through SC-08; all narrative choices are otherwise unchanged. Version-specific portal, fire, wolf and anchor behaviour still needs evidence, and Moss’s R4 fate remains intentionally open.","document":"# Story plan — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n> This is an unlocked Story proposal. It records no creative lock, audience result or production validation.\n\n## Development status\n\nThis revision adopts the new echo-reality journey and replaces the previous fixed-timeline tragedy within the unlocked development material.\n\nThe protagonist and Moss arrive together at a clearing above a ravine. Beneath it, they discover an active portal made from crying obsidian, although such a frame should not work like an ordinary Minecraft portal. The first crossing confuses the protagonist. After exploring the same ravine in a changed reality, he turns back expecting the portal to undo the trip. Instead, it advances him and Moss into another version of the same place. Further crossings reveal that the gate is a one-way chain rather than a reversible door.\n\nA damaged version of the protagonist’s own survey journal, found in an abandoned echo, explains enough of the invented portal behaviour to support the climax. When the chain finally strands them in a habitable reality, they build a home beside the ravine. A wool rug placed too close to an open fireplace later catches fire while the protagonist is away collecting the last materials needed for a controlled return. He comes home too late and Moss is gone.\n\nThe protagonist uses the journal’s instructions to build a complete crying-obsidian frame in the ravine. He discharges a respawn anchor to wake it, knowing the activation is explosive but believing the frame will open before the blast reaches him. The explosion kills him. The portal lights a fraction too late.\n\nThe camera enters the active frame, rises through the corresponding underground reality and breaks through the surface into the exact scene presented after the cold open: the protagonist and Moss first reaching the clearing and deciding to stay. The portal he will soon discover already waits below them. The ending therefore closes the physical, emotional and presentational loop without claiming that the protagonist survived the blast.\n\nThe portal rules, fire geometry, target Minecraft edition and explosion staging remain proposed until human approval and reproducibility testing.\n\n## Audience promise\n\nA lone player and his wolf discover a portal nobody could have built in an untouched part of their private world. Crossing it does not take them to the Nether. It carries them through increasingly unsettling realities of the same ravine, and trying to go back only moves them farther from the world they knew.\n\nThe intended pleasure is cumulative recognition:\n\n- the same geography acquiring incompatible histories;\n- a return attempt becoming another departure;\n- crude marks proving that the realities are distinct rather than changing around him;\n- an older version of his own journal turning an abstract anomaly into a dangerous possibility;\n- a comfortable household detail becoming the fire’s ordinary cause;\n- the crying frame at the end becoming the impossible portal from the beginning;\n- the final ascent revealing that the apparent beginning was always the portal’s destination.\n\nThe central questions are:\n\n- Who made the crying-obsidian portal beneath the clearing?\n- Why does going back through it lead somewhere new?\n- Can the protagonist use what he learns to return to a reality where Moss is still with him?\n- Does opening a route matter if he cannot survive long enough to cross it?\n\nThe story would break its promise if the realities were interchangeable spectacle, if a hidden wizard or visible second player supplied the explanation, if the rug fire lacked a legible material path, if Moss were forgotten while the house burned, or if the final explosion were presented as a verified vanilla method for activating crying obsidian.\n\n## The selected knowledge source: The Weeping Atlas\n\nThe strongest working solution is not a generic ancient lore book. It is a weathered version of the protagonist’s own survey journal, found in the Archive Echo.\n\nAt the beginning, the protagonist carries a small field journal in which he records coordinates and draws a distinctive three-line symbol for ravines. In the Archive Echo, he finds an older journal with the same repaired corner, the same shorthand and the same hand. It contains observations apparently made by another version of him:\n\n- several plans of the identical ravine laid over one another;\n- a diagram showing that an unanchored crossing follows the next arrow, never the previous one;\n- the phrase **ONE FRAME, MANY MOUTHS**;\n- a full crying-obsidian frame connected to a respawn anchor;\n- an arrow from that frame to **FIRST THRESHOLD**;\n- a warning that the hand which discharges the anchor becomes the key;\n- a blast-distance page whose final measurement has been torn away.\n\nThe book does not explain the universe or introduce a lore authority. Its diagrams record experiments, failed returns and practical observations. The protagonist verifies the one-way rule through his own crossings before trusting the final instruction.\n\nThe final surface reveal shows the younger protagonist carrying the clean, mostly blank version of the same journal. This pays off the book’s identity while leaving the exact history of the counterpart who completed it open. That unanswered authorship is reflective residue, not missing information required to understand the fire, the explosion or the loop.\n\n### Reserve approaches if the Atlas proves too explicit\n\n| Option | Dramatic advantage | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| An abandoned echo laboratory | Item frames, marked portals and redstone indicators could demonstrate the rule without prose | Requires a larger set and implies an unseen experimenter without the personal journal pay-off |\n| A cartographic palimpsest | Layered maps reveal the same coordinates and crying particles trace a route to the first map | More mysterious and visually elegant, but the respawn-anchor danger may become too obscure |\n| A ruined-portal field manual | A concise written book gives the rule directly | Cheapest to stage, but least personal and most dependent on exposition |\n\nThe Weeping Atlas is the working choice because it joins information, character identity, visual evidence and the final image without adding another speaking role.\n\n## Proposed echo-portal canon\n\nThese are invented story rules, not claims about vanilla Minecraft or real physics.\n\n1. Echoes are stable realities with the same underlying coordinates but divergent histories. They are not resets, dreams or temporary illusions.\n2. A crying-obsidian frame binds matching coordinates across echoes. The nearest safe vertical exit may differ, but the ravine profile remains the primary orientation landmark.\n3. An unanchored crossing moves forwards through a chain of echoes. Turning round and entering again does not reverse the previous crossing.\n4. Blocks placed beside a portal belong only to that reality. A missing cobblestone mark therefore provides fair evidence that the traveller has not returned.\n5. A projected mouth can appear in an echo where nobody locally placed the frame. Its material source is the constructed frame in the reality that created the connection.\n6. A complete crying-obsidian frame can be keyed to the builder’s first threshold by discharging a charged respawn anchor within it.\n7. The builder must trigger that discharge directly. The proposed Overworld discharge is explosive; the frame’s response to it is speculative canon.\n8. Activation does not rewrite a reality. It opens a route to another existing echo at the moment bound to the first threshold.\n9. The final R4 frame projects the receiving mouth found in R0. The R0 protagonist enters that receiving mouth without a key, so the one-way chain carries him onwards to R1 rather than back to R4.\n10. The camera may cross the completed connection after the protagonist dies, but this is audience access rather than evidence that his consciousness survives.\n\nThe film should teach only the rules needed for the current decision. Geography, repeated portal composition, adjacent block marks and the Atlas diagrams carry most of the explanation.\n\n## Dramatic opening\n\n### Cold open\n\nAt night, the protagonist returns over the ridge carrying crying obsidian and glowstone. His house is already burning. He drops the materials, runs past recoverable storage and empties his water bucket towards the shared room. The wool rug beside the fireplace is aflame. Moss cannot be seen from the doorway.\n\nThe protagonist breaks through the shortest wall and reaches into the smoke. The scene cuts before the room is revealed.\n\nA restrained narration line may establish the emotional promise:\n\n**By the time I saw the smoke, Moss had been alone in it for too long.**\n\nThe fragment should last about thirty-five seconds. It withholds Moss’s fate but makes the protagonist’s priority unambiguous.\n\n### Return to the beginning\n\nMorning replaces the fire. The protagonist and Moss reach a green clearing above a recognisable ravine. They are already an established pair: Moss follows naturally, waits while the protagonist checks the map and sits at the overlook while he opens his clean survey journal.\n\nThe repeated narration line is simple:\n\n**Moss sat down before I did. I took it as a vote. We could stay.**\n\nHe draws the ravine symbol, places a temporary marker and hears a low portal hum below. Their decision to investigate produces the first crossing. At this point, the audience reads the scene as an ordinary chronological beginning. The ending later reveals it as the receiving reality created by the final portal.\n\n## Protagonist’s personal journey and audience presentation\n\n1. **Fire without an answer:** The cold open shows the protagonist returning too late to an established home and racing towards an unseen Moss.\n2. **A place worth keeping:** Earlier in his experience, he and Moss reach the clearing, mark it in the survey journal and decide to stay.\n3. **The portal nobody made:** In the ravine they find a complete, active crying-obsidian frame in an unvisited private world. No path, mine, camp or dropped inventory identifies a builder.\n4. **The first crossing:** They enter together and emerge beside the same granite seam in the Rain Echo. The weather, vegetation and water paths are incompatible with the clearing they left.\n5. **The failed return:** Confused, the protagonist explores long enough to verify the geography, then re-enters the portal expecting to go home. It sends them to the Ash Echo instead.\n6. **A method replaces panic:** He marks the frame’s left foot with cobblestone, records coordinates and crosses once more. The next mouth has no mark. Same place and different histories become the best explanation.\n7. **The Weeping Atlas:** In the Archive Echo, an abandoned survey recess contains the older version of his journal. Its diagrams name the one-way behaviour and propose a dangerous method of reaching the first threshold.\n8. **The last uncontrolled crossing:** Environmental pressure and a failing portal plane force one more entry. They emerge in a habitable echo. The local mouth closes and cannot be relit with ordinary fire. Without a complete frame, sufficient crying obsidian or a charged anchor, they cannot leave.\n9. **A life in one reality:** They remain near the ravine because it is the only known seam between echoes. A shelter becomes a house. The protagonist builds a stone fireplace with an open flame and places a wool rug beside it for Moss. Their routines turn enforced settlement into home.\n10. **Preparing a route without abandoning the present:** Over time, the protagonist collects crying obsidian and studies the Atlas. He delays the dangerous activation until he has a full frame, glowstone and a plausible blast shelter.\n11. **The absence that completes the cold open:** He leaves Moss sitting on the warm rug during a final supply run through a conventional Nether route. Fire spreads from the open hearth to the wool while he is away. He returns to the cold open, reaches the room and finds Moss gone. He searches the ruins and surrounding paths but receives no response.\n12. **Grief turns research into urgency:** The protagonist traces the burn fan back to the missing rug, retrieves the materials he brought home and descends to the ravine. The Atlas’s first-threshold diagram now appears to offer the only reality in which Moss is certainly alive.\n13. **The final frame:** He builds a complete crying-obsidian frame at the dead mouth’s coordinates, places and charges a respawn anchor, and constructs an incomplete stone baffle. The torn safety page leaves him with evidence of danger but no reliable distance.\n14. **A route opened too late:** He directly discharges the anchor. The explosion kills him before the portal plane forms. Purple light appears through the smoke after his death.\n15. **The beginning as destination:** The camera enters, rises through corresponding stone and soil, and reaches the surface in R0. The exact earlier shot continues: the protagonist opens his blank journal, Moss sits at the overlook, and they decide to stay. The portal waits beneath them, completing the loop.\n\n## Reality chronology\n\n| Reality | Distinguishing state | Story function |\n|---|---|---|\n| R0 — Root Echo | Green clearing, dry ravine, no local player construction above ground; the projected crying portal waits below | The apparent beginning and final destination |\n| R1 — Rain Echo | Persistent rain, a new waterfall and dense vegetation, but identical stone and granite profiles | Creates confusion and invites the mistaken return attempt |\n| R2 — Ash Echo | A naturally scorched tree line and exposed ravine shelf; the prior cobblestone portal mark is absent | Confirms forward movement into distinct realities |\n| R3 — Archive Echo | A small abandoned survey recess contains the Weeping Atlas; portal behaviour becomes unstable | Supplies incomplete but actionable knowledge |\n| R4 — Home Echo | Habitable surface, inert local mouth and sufficient resources for long-term survival | Holds the house, wool-rug fire, final frame, death and outbound link to R0 |\n\nThe realities need not share a synchronised clock. The final keyed portal selects the R0 moment associated with the protagonist’s first threshold. It does not change an event already shown.\n\n## Plot and causality\n\nThe major chain is:\n\n- The clearing offers shelter and the portal hum threatens the protagonist’s confidence that the region is untouched, so he investigates.\n- The complete crying frame presents the only direct explanation for its own impossibility, so he enters with Moss rather than leaving the wolf behind.\n- The first changed reality confuses him, but repeated geography makes the portal the most credible route home.\n- Re-entering advances instead of returning, so he begins marking coordinates and adjacent blocks.\n- Missing marks and incompatible world histories establish a chain of echoes, so the Atlas can clarify observed evidence rather than invent the premise late.\n- The Atlas and failing mouth force a final uncontrolled crossing, which strands them in R4.\n- Staying near the only known seam leads to the house, hearth and wool rug.\n- The protagonist’s gradual portal research requires crying obsidian and glowstone, causing the supply journey during which the continuously burning hearth reaches the rug.\n- Sitting Moss indoors is an established safety choice for dangerous travel, but it prevents following or teleporting while the protagonist is away.\n- The fire and Moss’s disappearance turn a cautious future project into an immediate attempt to reach the first threshold.\n- The Atlas’s direct-hand rule requires the protagonist to remain close to the respawn anchor discharge.\n- The explosion kills him, but its discharge activates the crying frame.\n- The portal’s destination reveals the opening reality and creates the mouth his earlier self will enter.\n\nRemoving the failed return removes the central portal rule. Removing the marks makes the reality conclusion arbitrary. Removing the Atlas removes the protagonist’s basis for building and activating the final frame. Removing the domestic Moss scenes makes the rug and loss merely functional. Removing the wool adjacency makes the fire arbitrary. Removing the material-gathering objective removes the reason he is away. Removing the anchor blast breaks the protagonist’s death and the portal’s activation. Removing the repeated surface shot breaks the final loop.\n\n## Characters and relationship\n\n### The protagonist\n\nThe protagonist is observant, practical and accustomed to treating Minecraft systems as legible. He begins with healthy curiosity: when the portal contradicts his map, he investigates. His first mistake is understandable rather than foolish. He assumes that a portal can be traversed in reverse because ordinary doors and familiar Nether travel support that expectation.\n\nHis strength is evidence gathering. After the failed return, he marks blocks, records coordinates and compares terrain. His limitation emerges gradually: once knowledge offers even a dangerous possibility of reversal, he finds it harder to accept the reality in front of him.\n\nHis important decisions are to stay at the clearing, enter with Moss, attempt the return, continue marking echoes, trust the Atlas only after verifying part of it, settle near the dead mouth, build a home, pursue the materials slowly, leave Moss somewhere normally safe, search after the fire, build the crying frame and directly discharge the anchor.\n\nHis final choice is not between Moss and the house. The house is already lost. He chooses a lethal chance of reaching a reality with Moss over surviving in a reality where Moss may be gone.\n\n### Moss\n\nMoss is already the protagonist’s tamed companion when the story begins. The relationship is expressed through Minecraft-scale behaviour rather than human reasoning:\n\n- Moss follows while the protagonist surveys the clearing;\n- waits while he bridges or enters hazardous areas;\n- interrupts construction by occupying useful blocks;\n- receives food after ordinary damage;\n- sleeps or sits on the wool rug beside the hearth;\n- remains home only during the dangerous final supply run.\n\nMoss is not kept in a pen, kennel or separate pet room. The rug is part of the shared living space. Its placement begins as an affectionate domestic choice and becomes the fire’s vulnerable material.\n\nThe film confirms that Moss is absent after the fire but does not show a body, invented collar drop or on-screen death message. The protagonist treats that absence as loss. The final R0 image shows an earlier Moss, not proof that the R4 Moss escaped.\n\n### Opposition and pressure\n\nThere is no conventional villain. Active pressure comes from incompatible objectives within the protagonist:\n\n- his practical self wants evidence, preparation and a survivable activation;\n- his grief-driven self wants the first available route to a reality where Moss is alive.\n\nThe echo system has a one-way advantage. The Atlas is incomplete. The fire has destroyed time, shelter and emotional patience. Direct activation keeps the protagonist inside the blast problem. He could delay, rebuild, seek better protection or live without certainty, but every delay feels like choosing the Moss-less reality.\n\nThe costs remain concrete. Careful preparation costs emotional acceptance of the present. Immediate activation costs his life.\n\n## Movement of the story\n\n| Movement | Temporary objective and strategy | Reversal | Changed state |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. A door beneath home | Survive the cold-open promise, then inspect the clearing and impossible frame | The portal leads to a changed version of the same ravine | Chosen home becomes unexplained displacement |\n| 2. Every way is forwards | Explore, turn back and then mark successive mouths | The return attempt advances them, and the chain finally strands them | Confusion becomes knowledge without escape |\n| 3. Make one echo home | Settle near the seam, build a safe life and gather return materials patiently | A wool rug beside the open hearth creates an unnoticed fire route | Enforced settlement becomes valued home and latent danger |\n| 4. Come back too late | Reach Moss, search the ruins and determine the fire’s cause | Moss is gone and the materials for the dangerous route have just arrived | Care becomes grief-driven urgency |\n| 5. Wake the first threshold | Build the full crying frame and survive its activation | The anchor discharge kills him before the portal forms | Failed return becomes the portal found at the beginning |\n\nThis five-movement shape is a clarity tool for the particular chain of realities, not a mandatory formula or timing quota.\n\n## Scene progression\n\n| Scene | Entry and objective | Visual conflict or clue | Turn and outcome | Production need |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1. Smoke on the ridge | Return home and reach Moss | Burning rug, ignored supplies and an unseen shared room | He breaks in; cut before the room is revealed | Controlled partial fire state and matched continuation |\n| 2. A place to stay | Survey the clearing with Moss | Ravine symbol, blank journal and faint hum | They choose the clearing as home and descend | Exact shot reserved for the ending |\n| 3. Nobody built this | Explain the active crying frame | Unvisited coordinates and no construction trail | The protagonist enters with Moss | Crying-frame hero set and wolf transition blocking |\n| 4. The wrong way back | Orient in the Rain Echo and return | Same granite seam, incompatible water and vegetation | Re-entry produces the Ash Echo | Matched geography in two readable states |\n| 5. Marks that do not follow | Test whether the world is changing or being replaced | A cobblestone foot mark is absent at the next mouth | Distinct realities become the working model | Repeatable adjacent-block continuity |\n| 6. The Weeping Atlas | Search an abandoned survey recess | Older version of his journal and one-way diagrams | He learns of the full frame and first threshold | Custom book pages and concise insert shots |\n| 7. The last mouth closes | Reach a safer echo before the portal fails | Flickering field and dwindling supplies | They arrive in R4 and cannot relight the mouth | Inert frame state and clear reason immediate travel ends |\n| 8. A house beside the seam | Build a life while preparing slowly | Open hearth, wool rug and Moss’s preferred sitting place | Temporary shelter becomes home | Lived construction, feeding and sit-follow routines |\n| 9. One last supply run | Obtain final crying obsidian and glowstone | Moss stays on the rug beside a fire assumed to be contained | The protagonist leaves and the house remains vulnerable | Conventional portal route and material continuity |\n| 10. The room from the opening | Reach Moss and understand the fire | Wool-to-timber spread and an empty sitting place | Search fails; he chooses the Atlas route | Tested fire stages and no invented remains |\n| 11. One frame, many mouths | Build the full crying frame in the ravine | Exact opening geometry and incomplete blast protection | He charges the respawn anchor | Crying-obsidian inventory and coordinate match |\n| 12. The hand that wakes it | Activate the frame and cross | Direct interaction keeps him close to the discharge | Explosion kills him; portal lights afterwards | Version-tested blast staging plus fictional portal effect |\n| 13. Under the beginning | Reveal the portal’s destination | Camera rises through matching ground layers | R0 protagonist and Moss choose to stay above the active mouth | Stylised through-ground move and exact surface match |\n\n## Environmental clue design\n\n| Early detail | Initial reading | Later cause or pay-off |\n|---|---|---|\n| The same lightning-shaped granite seam | Useful orientation inside a strange destination | Confirms every crossing shares the same base geography |\n| A cobblestone block beside the R1 mouth | A practical route marker | Its absence in R2 proves adjacent blocks do not travel between realities |\n| Crying particles moving in one direction | Portal decoration or atmosphere | The Atlas diagrams identify the one-way echo current |\n| The clean survey journal | Ordinary expedition bookkeeping | The Archive Echo contains an older counterpart with matching symbols |\n| The phrase one frame, many mouths | Abstract portal theory | The final constructed frame projects the mouth found in R0 |\n| A torn blast-distance page | Damage to an old book | Leaves the protagonist with a real warning but no safe activation measurement |\n| Moss repeatedly choosing the wool rug | Domestic warmth and visual character | The familiar sitting place becomes the centre of the fire and later absence |\n| Stone surrounding the fireplace | Evidence of responsible construction | The stone survives, but the wool remains within the open flame’s spread range |\n| Crying obsidian and glowstone brought home | Hope of eventual travel | They explain why the protagonist was away and supply the final frame and anchor |\n| The cold open’s dropped materials | Unexplained purple cargo | The completed story reveals the final supply run and immediate portal capability |\n| The first clearing composition | A chronological restart after the cold open | The final ascent reveals it as the portal’s selected destination |\n| The blank journal visible in the final shot | A repeated personal prop | Suggests how another echo of the protagonist could eventually create the Atlas |\n\n## Ending and meaning\n\nThe informational climax is the activation of the full crying frame. The protagonist has physically built the portal, supplied the crying obsidian, charged the anchor and triggered the event that projects its earlier mouth. Nobody built the portal in R0, but the protagonist built the connected frame in R4 after he had already found it in his own personal order.\n\nThe emotional climax is his decision to wake it before he has a verified way to survive. He is not careless about Moss or distracted by possessions. He has searched, found the rug empty and concluded that the only reality in which Moss is certainly alive lies behind the first threshold. The tragedy is that the method works a fraction too late for its builder.\n\nAfter the blast, the camera remains. Purple light gathers between the crying blocks. It passes through the frame, travels vertically through dark stone and soil, and emerges into morning.\n\nThe exact Return to the beginning composition appears. Moss sits at the overlook. The earlier protagonist opens the blank journal and draws the ravine symbol. The earlier narration repeats:\n\n**Moss sat down before I did. I took it as a vote. We could stay.**\n\nA faint portal hum rises from below. The audience knows they will investigate, enter, attempt to return, cross the echoes, build the house and eventually create the connection beneath them.\n\nThe ending argues that reaching another reality is not the same as recovering what was lost. The protagonist succeeds in opening the route but cannot turn that technical success into a second life. Home remains meaningful because he and Moss chose and made it together, even though that choice also begins the loop.\n\nMoss’s exact fate in R4 remains intentionally unseen in this proposal. If Story lock requires explicit death rather than absence, that must be a human decision and the fire blocking should be revised without inventing a non-Minecraft corpse or keepsake.\n\n## Minecraft story engine\n\n### Candidate ordinary mechanics to verify\n\n- A tamed wolf can follow the protagonist through the planned portal blocking or be transferred through controlled capture.\n- A sitting wolf remains in place rather than following or teleporting during the supply run.\n- An open fireplace source can ignite wool placed within the relevant fire-spread range when fire spread is enabled.\n- Fire can continue from the rug into adjacent timber and create the planned house state.\n- Crying obsidian and glowstone can be acquired through the chosen survival route in the target edition and version.\n- A charged respawn anchor can produce the required Overworld explosion, damage and surviving frame geometry.\n\nThese behaviours are production candidates, not claimed test results.\n\n### Invented mechanics\n\n- Crying-obsidian frames can form active portals.\n- Such portals connect echo-realities rather than the Nether.\n- Unanchored re-entry advances rather than returns.\n- A directly discharged respawn anchor can key a full crying frame to the traveller’s first threshold.\n- The discharge activates the portal after the explosion.\n- A constructed frame can project a receiving mouth into another reality at matching coordinates.\n\nThe film must not present those rules as vanilla facts. The Atlas, repeated crossings and distinct visual language establish them as this story’s speculative canon.\n\n## Narration and camera\n\n- One narrator carries the protagonist’s interpretation; no additional spoken role is required.\n- The protagonist remains silent on screen.\n- The narrator may say what he believed at each stage but cannot call the echoes timelines after evidence supports separate realities.\n- The Atlas should be read through diagrams and a few short labels, not a long spoken lecture.\n- The cold open withholds Moss’s fate by cutting, not by making a false statement.\n- The final through-ground camera is explicitly omniscient audience presentation after the protagonist’s death.\n- No visible counterpart, double-avatar conversation or explanatory authority is required.\n\n## Production scope and evidence still required\n\nThe working target remains approximately twenty minutes, with clarity taking priority over a fixed duration.\n\nThe smallest version worth making requires:\n\n- one ravine reproduced in five strongly differentiated echo states;\n- one active crying-frame state, one unstable state, one inert state and one final activation state;\n- one compact house in lived, burning and burned conditions;\n- one protagonist avatar and one wolf;\n- one custom journal prop with a small number of legible pages;\n- one conventional supply route for crying obsidian and glowstone;\n- one controlled explosion and one stylised vertical portal transition.\n\nBefore Story lock, testing or authoritative version-specific evidence is still needed for:\n\n- wolf portal transfer and sit-state continuity;\n- fireplace-to-wool ignition distance;\n- fire spread from rug to house structure;\n- simulation-distance or chunk-loading behaviour during the protagonist’s absence;\n- the time available between return and the completed burn state;\n- respawn-anchor acquisition, charging, explosion radius and lethal damage;\n- frame survival and camera blocking after the explosion;\n- separation between verified Minecraft behaviour and the invented activation effect;\n- exact repeated coordinates and lenses across all realities;\n- readability of the Atlas inserts at the intended viewing size;\n- whether Moss’s absence communicates loss without requiring an explicit death image.\n\nNo audience test, animatic result, mechanical reproduction, capture validation or final production imagery is claimed.","files":[{"path":".studio/internal/story/story-model.json","content":"{\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"audience_promise\": {\n    \"novel_situation\": \"A lone Minecraft player and his tamed wolf find an active crying-obsidian portal beneath an untouched clearing, discover that every attempted return advances them through another reality of the same ravine, and eventually become stranded in the reality where the player will build the connected portal himself.\",\n    \"human_problem\": \"The protagonist learns how to reach his first threshold only after a wool-rug fire leaves Moss missing, turning a dangerous possibility of return into an urgent refusal to remain in the present reality.\",\n    \"pressure_source\": \"A one-way echo chain, dwindling supplies, an incomplete journal, a dead local portal mouth, a flammable domestic detail, Moss's sit state and a directly triggered respawn-anchor discharge compress his choices.\",\n    \"expected_pleasure\": [\n      \"Recognising one ravine beneath incompatible reality states\",\n      \"Watching an attempted return become another departure\",\n      \"Reading portal rules through marks, geography and the protagonist's echoed journal\",\n      \"Seeing a warm household detail become the ordinary cause of the fire\",\n      \"Discovering that the final crying frame projects the portal found at the beginning\",\n      \"Reinterpreting the Return to the beginning scene as the final portal's destination\"\n    ],\n    \"disappointment_test\": \"The promise fails if the realities are decorative repetitions, a visible second player explains the portal, the Atlas replaces observed evidence, the rug fire lacks a readable material path, the protagonist protects property instead of Moss, or speculative crying-obsidian behaviour is presented as verified vanilla Minecraft.\"\n  },\n  \"premise\": {\n    \"disruption\": \"The protagonist and Moss discover a complete active crying-obsidian frame beneath an unvisited clearing, and trying to return through it sends them onwards into another reality.\",\n    \"protagonist_id\": \"PROTAGONIST\",\n    \"difficult_goal\": \"Find a survivable route back to the first reality while keeping Moss safe and making a life in the echo where the portal strands them.\",\n    \"central_opposition\": \"His evidence-led patience conflicts with his grief-driven demand for immediate reversal while the one-way portal rule, fire and explosive activation make haste consequential.\",\n    \"meaningful_consequence\": \"The material-gathering plan leaves Moss beside a flammable rug during the fire, and the protagonist's final attempt opens the route to the beginning only after its explosion kills him.\",\n    \"logline\": \"After a crying-obsidian portal traps a Minecraft player and his wolf in a chain of alternate realities, they build a home beside its dead mouth; when a wool rug catches fire and Moss disappears, the player follows an echoed version of his own journal to construct a return portal whose activation kills him but creates the impossible doorway he first found.\"\n  },\n  \"theme\": {\n    \"subject\": \"Grief, home and the difference between a route back and a second chance\",\n    \"question\": \"If another reality still contains what was lost, does opening a path to it restore a life or merely refuse the life that remains?\",\n    \"provisional_argument\": \"Technical access to another possibility cannot undo the cost already paid, and grief can turn a real route into a demand for immediate action that the traveller cannot survive.\",\n    \"competing_values\": [\n      \"Care expressed through inhabiting the present\",\n      \"Control pursued through another reality\",\n      \"Patient evidence and survivable preparation\",\n      \"Urgent hope after ambiguous loss\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"characters\": [\n    {\n      \"character_id\": \"PROTAGONIST\",\n      \"role\": \"protagonist\",\n      \"want\": \"Understand the crying portal, recover the first reality and keep Moss beside him.\",\n      \"need\": \"Distinguish a possible route from a guaranteed second chance and recognise when grief has replaced evidence as his decision standard.\",\n      \"fear\": \"That every crossing takes him farther from the only reality where he and Moss were unquestionably safe.\",\n      \"belief\": \"If he can record enough coordinates, acquire the correct materials and reproduce the Atlas diagram, Minecraft systems will remain legible and controllable.\",\n      \"limitation\": \"After Moss disappears, he treats delay for safer activation as equivalent to abandoning the wolf.\",\n      \"strategy\": \"Compare persistent geography, mark portal mouths, study the echoed journal, remain near the ravine seam, gather crying obsidian and glowstone, and key a full frame with a respawn anchor.\",\n      \"contradiction\": \"His patient routines create a genuine home with Moss, but the possibility of reversal eventually makes that patience intolerable.\",\n      \"boundary\": \"Once the fire is visible, he abandons every recoverable possession and directs each action towards finding Moss.\",\n      \"pressure_point\": \"He returns to the burning home with the final portal materials and finds Moss's familiar wool-rug position empty.\",\n      \"relationship_function\": \"Moss turns the echo chain from a spatial mystery into a shared life whose possible loss makes the first threshold irresistible.\",\n      \"agency\": \"He chooses the clearing, enters the portal, attempts the return, records successive echoes, uses the Atlas, settles near the seam, builds the house, gathers the activation materials, searches for Moss, constructs the final frame and directly discharges the anchor.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"character_id\": \"WOLF\",\n      \"role\": \"companion\",\n      \"want\": \"Expressed behaviourally, to follow the protagonist, remain near the shared home and occupy the warm wool rug.\",\n      \"need\": \"The protagonist must keep its command state and environmental safety compatible when he leaves the house.\",\n      \"fear\": \"Expressed only through ordinary avoidance, distress and fire response rather than humanised thought.\",\n      \"belief\": \"No abstract belief is assigned; Moss follows when standing and waits when seated.\",\n      \"limitation\": \"Moss cannot interpret the portal diagrams, assess the fireplace or cancel the sit command during the protagonist's absence.\",\n      \"strategy\": \"Follow across safe terrain, wait during hazardous work, accept food after damage and settle on the shared rug.\",\n      \"contradiction\": \"The reliable waiting behaviour that normally protects Moss prevents following or teleporting away when the house catches fire.\",\n      \"boundary\": \"Moss is never placed in a pen, kennel, separate pet room or human-like decision role.\",\n      \"pressure_point\": \"The protagonist leaves for the final material run while Moss is sitting beside the open hearth.\",\n      \"relationship_function\": \"Moss makes each reality emotionally comparable and turns enforced settlement into a chosen home.\",\n      \"agency\": \"Limited diegetic agency: Moss follows, sits, obstructs construction, accepts food and chooses familiar resting positions, but cannot override the final command state.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"opposition\": {\n    \"opposing_goal\": \"The protagonist's grief-driven objective seeks immediate access to the first threshold, while his practical survival objective requires delay, testing and safer blast protection.\",\n    \"why_neither_side_can_withdraw\": \"The first threshold is the only reality in which Moss is certainly alive, but the activation method requires direct exposure and the protagonist has no complete safety measurement.\",\n    \"advantage\": \"The echo route is one-way, the Atlas is incomplete, the house fire has compressed his emotional judgement and the direct-hand rule places him beside the explosive anchor.\",\n    \"vulnerability\": \"He could wait, obtain stronger protection, test a sacrificial frame, search longer for Moss or accept uncertainty in R4.\",\n    \"cost_of_confrontation\": \"Attempting activation immediately risks the protagonist's life and destroys any chance to continue searching in R4.\",\n    \"cost_of_avoidance\": \"Delaying means remaining in the reality where Moss is absent and accepting that another echo may be reachable but not recoverable.\"\n  },\n  \"stakes\": {\n    \"immediate\": \"Keep Moss with him and determine whether the first changed ravine can be exited safely.\",\n    \"practical\": \"Each crossing consumes supplies and may strand them in a less habitable reality without a working mouth.\",\n    \"personal\": \"The protagonist's trust that patient observation can make every Minecraft system controllable is tested by incomplete cross-reality evidence.\",\n    \"relational\": \"Moss is left sitting beside the flammable rug and is gone when the protagonist returns.\",\n    \"moral\": \"The fire response must prioritise Moss completely; the tragic error lies in prior material placement and later activation haste, not preference for property.\",\n    \"existential\": \"Other realities make another possibility visible without guaranteeing that the traveller can inhabit it.\",\n    \"systemic\": \"The consequences remain within one player, one wolf, five echoes and one repeated ravine.\"\n  },\n  \"story_layers\": {\n    \"chronological_story\": \"In R0 the protagonist and Moss choose a clearing and enter a projected crying portal. Unanchored crossings carry them through R1, R2 and R3 into R4, where they build a home. A wool rug catches fire during a material run and Moss disappears. The protagonist builds and activates a keyed crying frame in R4; its explosion kills him while its receiving mouth opens into the earlier R0 threshold.\",\n    \"causal_plot\": \"An impossible portal prompts entry; a failed return prompts systematic marking; the marks make the Atlas credible; the chain's collapse prompts settlement; settlement creates the hearth and rug; portal preparation causes the absence during the fire; loss prompts immediate construction; direct activation causes death and opens the beginning mouth.\",\n    \"audience_presentation\": \"A fragment of the R4 fire opens the film, followed by the R0 arrival, portal discovery, successive realities, Atlas discovery, R4 home life, completed fire, Moss's absence, final construction, death and a through-ground return to the repeated R0 arrival.\"\n  },\n  \"ending_design\": {\n    \"final_choice\": \"Directly discharge the respawn anchor and attempt the first-threshold connection before obtaining a verified survival method.\",\n    \"climax_mechanism\": \"The protagonist keys a complete crying-obsidian frame through a direct Overworld respawn-anchor discharge; the explosion kills him, after which the invented portal field forms and projects its receiving mouth into R0.\",\n    \"emotional_result\": \"He succeeds in opening a reality where Moss is alive but cannot survive to enter it, while the audience sees the earlier pair unknowingly approach the portal beneath them.\",\n    \"thematic_consequence\": \"A route to another possibility is not the same as recovering a lost life, and successful control of a mechanism cannot erase the cost of the choice that activated it.\",\n    \"final_image\": \"After the protagonist dies in R4, the camera passes through the newly lit crying portal, rises through matching stone and soil into R0 morning, and reveals the earlier protagonist and Moss at the ravine overlook opening the blank journal and choosing to stay above the receiving mouth.\",\n    \"final_state\": \"The protagonist is dead in R4, Moss's R4 fate remains unseen, the crying portal is active, and R0 protagonist and Moss have just chosen the clearing above its receiving mouth as home.\"\n  },\n  \"structure_choice\": {\n    \"framework\": \"Five-movement echo-loop mystery with an out-of-order fire fragment and a terminal return to the opening reality\",\n    \"rationale\": \"The movements separate discovery, failed return, settlement, loss and activation so each reality crossing changes knowledge or forces a decision. The framework is a clarity aid for this material, not a fixed beat or duration formula.\"\n  },\n  \"minecraft_story_engine\": {\n    \"mechanic\": \"Repeated coordinates, player-placed block marks, wolf sit-follow behaviour, a flammable wool rug, crying-obsidian acquisition and a candidate respawn-anchor explosion drive the plot, while the crying-frame connection is explicit speculative canon.\",\n    \"causal_effect\": \"Minecraft persistence lets the protagonist distinguish echoes; construction creates the domestic fire route; survival gathering explains his absence and final materials; the direct activation requirement makes grief-driven haste physically lethal.\",\n    \"climactic_use\": \"The protagonist builds the full crying frame in the ravine, directly discharges the charged anchor, dies in the explosion and leaves an active portal whose far mouth is revealed beneath the beginning clearing.\"\n  }\n}"},{"path":".studio/internal/story/causal-spine.csv","content":"event_id,order,summary,cause_ids,cause_type,actor_id,goal,decision,consequence,state_change,new_information,audience_effect,dependent_event_ids,counterfactual_result,deletion_impact,production_note\n\"CE-01\",\"1\",\"The protagonist and Moss reach an untouched clearing above an unvisited ravine, choose it as a possible home and discover an active crying-obsidian frame below.\",\"\",\"initial_state\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Establish a safe stopping place and account for the impossible portal hum.\",\"He records the clearing in his survey journal and descends with Moss instead of ignoring the contradiction.\",\"The pair confront a constructed threshold with no local builder or approach route.\",\"Routine exploration becomes a shared authorship mystery.\",\"A crying-obsidian portal is active where no ordinary portal should exist.\",\"Warmth from the apparent beginning acquires an immediate layer of unease.\",\"CE-02|CE-10\",\"pass\",\"Without the chosen clearing and impossible frame, there is no threshold, title question or surface image for the ending to reinterpret.\",\"Reserve the exact surface composition, blank journal, ravine seam and portal cave for later matches.\"\n\"CE-02\",\"2\",\"They enter the portal, explore the Rain Echo and re-enter expecting to return, but arrive in the Ash Echo instead.\",\"CE-01\",\"prior_event\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Verify the destination and take Moss back to the clearing they left.\",\"After matching the ravine geography, he treats the same frame as a reversible door and crosses again.\",\"The attempted return becomes a second displacement.\",\"Curious traveller becomes unwilling echo traveller.\",\"Re-entry does not undo a crying-portal crossing.\",\"Confusion sharpens into the story's governing threat.\",\"CE-03\",\"pass\",\"Deleting the failed return removes the user-requested reversal and leaves later one-way knowledge unearned.\",\"Use identical granite and stone profiles with clearly incompatible rain, water and surface states.\"\n\"CE-03\",\"3\",\"The protagonist marks successive mouths and confirms that each crossing enters a distinct reality of the same place.\",\"CE-02\",\"prior_event\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Replace panic with a testable model and avoid losing orientation.\",\"He places a cobblestone mark beside one frame, records coordinates and crosses when the current echo becomes unsafe.\",\"The next mouth has the same frame and coordinates but not the adjacent mark.\",\"Displacement becomes an evidence-led echo model.\",\"Portal-adjacent blocks belong to individual realities while the ravine geometry persists.\",\"The audience can anticipate further realities without treating them as arbitrary spectacle.\",\"CE-04\",\"pass\",\"Without the marking test, the Atlas supplies a rule the protagonist has not earned and the realities could be read as changing time states.\",\"Keep the mark outside the frame and repeat its absence in a clean matched insert.\"\n\"CE-04\",\"4\",\"In the Archive Echo he finds the Weeping Atlas, then takes one final uncontrolled crossing into R4 where the local mouth closes.\",\"CE-03\",\"prior_event\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Learn whether any crossing can reach the first threshold and escape the failing echo.\",\"He compares the older journal with his own, takes it and trusts its next-arrow warning when the portal destabilises.\",\"He and Moss reach a habitable reality but cannot relight the dead mouth with ordinary fire.\",\"Systematic traveller becomes informed but stranded settler.\",\"A full crying frame keyed by a direct respawn-anchor discharge may reach the traveller's first threshold.\",\"Explanation creates hope while the torn blast page preserves danger.\",\"CE-05|CE-08\",\"pass\",\"Deleting the Atlas removes the established crying-frame deviation, the final construction basis and the journal pay-off.\",\"Use a few legible diagrams, a matching journal prop and an inert R4 portal state; do not claim the rule is vanilla.\"\n\"CE-05\",\"5\",\"The protagonist and Moss settle beside the R4 ravine, build a house and establish a wool-rug routine beside an open fireplace while gathering portal materials slowly.\",\"CE-04\",\"prior_event\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Make a safe life without abandoning the possibility of a prepared return.\",\"He remains near the only known seam, builds a stone-surrounded hearth and places a wool rug where Moss prefers to sit.\",\"Enforced settlement becomes a valued home with a concealed flammable adjacency.\",\"Stranding becomes attachment and long-term preparation.\",\"The household seen burning in the cold open arises from ordinary shared choices.\",\"Relief and affection make the latent fire route emotionally costly.\",\"CE-06\",\"pass\",\"Without home life and the rug routine, the fire and Moss's absence are merely plot mechanisms.\",\"Capture construction, feeding, follow and sit behaviour; test the exact hearth-to-wool range before lock.\"\n\"CE-06\",\"6\",\"He leaves Moss sitting on the rug during a final material run, and fire spreads from the open hearth into the wool while he is away.\",\"CE-05\",\"prior_event\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Obtain the last crying obsidian and glowstone required for a full frame and anchor.\",\"He treats the stone fireplace as contained, leaves its permanent flame burning and keeps Moss home from the dangerous route.\",\"The rug ignites and carries fire into the timber interior before he returns.\",\"Patient portal preparation becomes immediate domestic danger.\",\"The fire comes from the affectionate rug placement and ordinary absence, not an echo force.\",\"The cold-open disaster becomes traceable before its completed outcome is shown.\",\"CE-07\",\"pass\",\"Without the supply decision and material path, the fire is arbitrary and his final inventory lacks an origin.\",\"Design the route or simulation conditions so the house can progress to the planned state; verify in the target version.\"\n\"CE-07\",\"7\",\"The protagonist returns, completes the cold-open rescue route and finds Moss gone despite searching the house and surrounding paths.\",\"CE-06\",\"prior_event\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Reach Moss without spending time on possessions or structural defence.\",\"He drops the portal materials, directs water and block breaking towards the shared room, then searches beyond the burned shell when the rug position is empty.\",\"The house is lost and Moss's R4 fate becomes an unresolved absence.\",\"Home and companionship become grief and uncertainty.\",\"The wool rug was the fire's first vulnerable block and Moss is no longer present in the reality the protagonist can inspect.\",\"The withheld cold-open outcome resolves as absence rather than an invented body or token.\",\"CE-08\",\"pass\",\"Deleting the failed search removes the emotional cause of immediate activation and risks making the protagonist abandon Moss too quickly.\",\"Stage the same cold-open actions, an empty sitting position and a credible external search; no death message or collar object.\"\n\"CE-08\",\"8\",\"Using the Atlas and the materials brought home, he builds a complete crying-obsidian frame at the R4 ravine seam.\",\"CE-04|CE-07\",\"parallel_cause\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Open a route to the first threshold where Moss is certainly alive.\",\"He stops treating the frame as a future project and reconstructs the Atlas geometry immediately after the search fails.\",\"The final frame reproduces the impossible portal composition from R0 and a charged anchor is placed within it.\",\"Grief becomes committed cross-reality action.\",\"The protagonist is the material builder of the connected portal found at the beginning.\",\"The title mystery resolves while activation danger remains.\",\"CE-09\",\"pass\",\"Without his construction choice, the initial portal has no dramatic author and the final materials have no pay-off.\",\"Show every crying block, the exact ravine coordinates, anchor placement and incomplete stone baffle.\"\n\"CE-09\",\"9\",\"The protagonist directly discharges the respawn anchor; the explosion kills him and the crying portal activates after the blast.\",\"CE-08\",\"prior_event\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Key the completed frame to his first threshold and cross before the discharge reaches him.\",\"He accepts the incomplete safety evidence and triggers the anchor by hand rather than delaying for a tested method.\",\"He dies before crossing, but the frame forms a stable portal plane through the smoke.\",\"Hopeful builder becomes absent builder; inert frame becomes active connection.\",\"The opening portal's activation and the protagonist's final absence share one chosen cause.\",\"Success and death arrive in the wrong order, completing the tragedy.\",\"CE-10\",\"pass\",\"Without the direct discharge, neither the explicit death nor the active final portal is caused by the protagonist's value choice.\",\"Verify candidate anchor blast behaviour separately from the fictional crying-frame activation effect.\"\n\"CE-10\",\"10\",\"The camera crosses the lit frame and rises into R0 as the earlier protagonist and Moss choose the clearing above it as home.\",\"CE-01|CE-09\",\"parallel_cause\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"In the revealed earlier moment, mark the new place and stay with Moss.\",\"The earlier protagonist opens his blank journal, records the ravine and chooses to investigate the hum below.\",\"The apparent beginning is identified as the final portal's receiving reality and the first decision leads towards the same journey.\",\"Linear presentation closes into a cross-reality loop.\",\"The R4 frame projects the R0 portal, the blank journal precedes the Atlas and the pair are about to find the threshold.\",\"Tragic death gives way to tenderness, recognition and inevitable continuation.\",\"\",\"pass\",\"Without the vertical reveal and repeated decision, the portal destination, journal image and requested loop remain incomplete.\",\"Use an omniscient through-ground transition, the exact SC-02 surface composition and repeated audio rather than implying player survival.\""},{"path":".studio/internal/story/sequence-outline.csv","content":"sequence_id,order,temporary_goal,strategy,primary_location,complications,reversal,outcome,story_state_afterward,estimated_seconds,production_note\n\"SQ-01\",\"1\",\"Survive the promised fire image, then understand the clearing and impossible portal beneath it.\",\"Open on direct rescue action, cut before the room reveal, return to the pair's arrival and let map evidence and portal hum pull them into the ravine.\",\"R4 burning house fragment, R0 clearing and R0 ravine cave\",\"The cold open supplies unexplained loss while the unvisited R0 frame contradicts the protagonist's authorship knowledge.\",\"The crying portal leads to the same ravine in a different reality.\",\"The protagonist and Moss cross by choice and lose their original context.\",\"A proposed home becomes a cross-reality mystery.\",\"160\",\"Reserve the exact clearing image for SQ-05 and show no local builder, message or second avatar.\"\n\"SQ-02\",\"2\",\"Get back to R0 and determine how the crying portal selects destinations.\",\"Explore the first echo, attempt a return, mark successive mouths, compare coordinates and use the Weeping Atlas only after observed evidence supports it.\",\"R1 Rain Echo, R2 Ash Echo, R3 Archive Echo and R4 dead mouth\",\"Each apparent return advances the chain, local marks disappear, supplies diminish and the Archive mouth destabilises.\",\"The Atlas offers a keyed return method, but the final uncontrolled crossing strands them in R4 without the necessary materials.\",\"Confusion becomes a reliable model and a long-term problem.\",\"Knowledge replaces panic, but knowledge cannot provide immediate escape.\",\"310\",\"Give every echo one strong state change and one informational function; keep Atlas inserts concise.\"\n\"SQ-03\",\"3\",\"Make R4 habitable while preparing a survivable first-threshold route.\",\"Stay beside the ravine seam, build a compact home, establish Moss's rug routine and acquire crying obsidian and glowstone gradually.\",\"R4 clearing, house, ravine and conventional supply route\",\"The local mouth is inert, the full frame is resource-intensive and the open hearth appears safely surrounded by stone.\",\"The final supply journey leaves Moss sitting beside wool within the fire-spread range.\",\"Stranding becomes home while the return plan creates the conditions for absence.\",\"Patient coexistence becomes latent domestic danger.\",\"265\",\"Use lived construction and survival actions; the fireplace-to-wool geometry remains provisional until tested.\"\n\"SQ-04\",\"4\",\"Return through the burning house, find Moss and determine whether any present-world action remains.\",\"Ignore supplies, direct water and block breaking towards the shared room, search the ruin and nearby terrain, then trace the burn fan to the rug.\",\"R4 burning house, burned clearing and ravine entrance\",\"Moss's sit state, progressed fire and lack of recoverable evidence deny a clear rescue or answer.\",\"Moss is gone, and the materials just brought home make the Atlas route immediately possible.\",\"The protagonist abandons gradual preparation and commits to the crying frame.\",\"Care becomes uncertain grief and urgent reversal.\",\"195\",\"Complete the cold-open blocking exactly; do not invent a corpse, collar drop or tested fire result.\"\n\"SQ-05\",\"5\",\"Build and wake a portal to the first threshold before accepting the R4 loss.\",\"Reproduce the full crying frame, charge the anchor, use an incomplete blast baffle and trigger the required direct discharge.\",\"R4 ravine portal cave, portal passage and R0 surface clearing\",\"The Atlas safety page is torn, direct interaction requires proximity and the protagonist is acting after the fire and failed search.\",\"The explosion kills him before the portal forms, but the active connection opens into the earlier R0 arrival.\",\"The builder mystery and destination resolve while the earlier pair unknowingly begin the same journey.\",\"Fatal activation becomes the portal that nobody made in R0.\",\"210\",\"Separate verified candidate explosion behaviour from fictional portal activation and use an omniscient final camera.\""},{"path":".studio/internal/story/beat-sheet.csv","content":"beat_id,sequence_id,order,actor_id,attempt,interference,change,next_cause,purpose,reveal,visual,audio,estimated_seconds,dependency\n\"BEAT-01\",\"SQ-01\",\"1\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Return through the burning house and reach Moss.\",\"The fire has already reached the wool rug and shared room while Moss is unseen.\",\"He drops valuable portal materials, sends water towards the room and breaks in before the film cuts.\",\"CE-07\",\"Open with loss, action and an honestly withheld outcome.\",\"The protagonist values Moss above the house and has returned carrying crying obsidian and glowstone.\",\"Purple materials hit the ground as water crosses the burning rug.\",\"Fire and block breaking overtake one short narration line.\",\"35\",\"SC-01\"\n\"BEAT-02\",\"SQ-01\",\"2\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Survey a new clearing and decide whether it can become home.\",\"The ravine is remote, but water, timber and shelter make it promising.\",\"Moss sits at the overlook, the protagonist records the place and decides they can stay.\",\"CE-01\",\"Establish the relationship, blank journal and surface image later reinterpreted by the ending.\",\"The pair arrive together and the ravine symbol belongs to the protagonist's ordinary notation.\",\"Morning wide shot, Moss at the ledge and a clean journal page.\",\"The repeated line about Moss's vote plays over quiet ambience.\",\"55\",\"SC-02\"\n\"BEAT-03\",\"SQ-01\",\"3\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Identify the portal hum and test the impossible frame.\",\"The crying-obsidian frame is complete and active despite no local path, mine or builder evidence.\",\"Observation cannot explain it, so he leads Moss through.\",\"CE-02\",\"Honour the portal promise through accountable curiosity.\",\"The private unvisited ravine contains a portal that cannot be an ordinary Nether frame.\",\"Hand, wolf and purple plane share the threshold before the transition.\",\"Portal hum replaces narration at the decision.\",\"70\",\"SC-03\"\n\"BEAT-04\",\"SQ-02\",\"4\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Orient in the Rain Echo and return to R0.\",\"The same stone profile exists beneath incompatible water, weather and vegetation.\",\"He explores, confirms the coordinates and re-enters the portal, only to emerge in the Ash Echo.\",\"CE-02\",\"Deliver the requested confusion, exploration and failed return.\",\"A crying portal is not reversible through immediate re-entry.\",\"The granite seam matches while a waterfall and scorched horizon replace the clearing.\",\"Rain cuts abruptly to dry wind after the second transition.\",\"85\",\"SC-04\"\n\"BEAT-05\",\"SQ-02\",\"5\",\"PROTAGONIST\",\"Test whether the destination is another reality or a changing version of one world.\",\"The frame persists, bu